WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.624
Browser data is usually organized around profiles.

2
00:00:03.624 --> 00:00:15.814
A browser profile is a collection of user-specific data such as history, cookies, settings, extensions, downloads, cache references, bookmarks, form data, and web storage.

3
00:00:15.814 --> 00:00:24.463
A single computer may have multiple operating system users, multiple browsers, and multiple profiles inside each browser.

4
00:00:24.463 --> 00:00:29.076
Finding the right profile is one of the first investigative tasks.

5
00:00:29.076 --> 00:00:35.006
Chrome, Edge, Firefox, and Safari all store profile data differently at a high level.

6
00:00:35.006 --> 00:00:44.890
Chromium-based browsers commonly use profile folders with databases, cache directories, extension data, preferences, and storage areas.

7
00:00:44.890 --> 00:00:47.608
Firefox has its own profile structure.

8
00:00:47.608 --> 00:00:51.809
Safari is integrated more closely with the Apple ecosystem.

9
00:00:51.809 --> 00:00:55.268
SQLite databases are common in browser analysis.

10
00:00:55.268 --> 00:01:05.235
They may store history, visits, downloads, cookies, favicons, form entries, or other structured artifacts depending on browser and version.

11
00:01:05.235 --> 00:01:10.341
Cache directories may contain web resources or references to resources.

12
00:01:10.341 --> 00:01:18.413
Extension folders and storage areas may contain configuration, permissions, and extension-specific artifacts.

13
00:01:18.413 --> 00:01:24.014
Each artifact should be tied back to its browser, profile, and platform context.

14
00:01:24.014 --> 00:01:28.792
Synced accounts and multiple profiles complicate interpretation.

15
00:01:28.792 --> 00:01:38.182
A browser may synchronize history, bookmarks, passwords, extensions, tabs, or settings across devices depending on configuration.

16
00:01:38.182 --> 00:01:42.876
A profile may be personal, corporate, temporary, guest, or shared.

17
00:01:42.876 --> 00:01:56.796
Private browsing may reduce or change local artifact retention, but it does not guarantee that no evidence exists elsewhere, such as network logs, DNS records, endpoint telemetry, or cloud services.

18
00:01:56.796 --> 00:02:04.621
Investigators should identify the correct user, profile, device, and time period before drawing conclusions.

19
00:02:04.621 --> 00:02:09.069
A finding in the wrong profile can be worse than no finding at all.

20
00:02:09.069 --> 00:02:23.319
Good profile analysis documents where the data came from, which browser and version created it, which user context was associated with it, and what synchronization or private-mode limitations may apply.
